{
  "esm": "1.2.0",
  "metadata": {
    "name": "GWDOWaveSaturation",
    "description": "The vertical structure of the orographic gravity-wave stress: how much of the launch stress taub survives to each interface as the wave propagates upward through shear and stratification. Lines 496-552 of `phys/physics_mmm/bl_gwdo.F90` (WRF 4.8, NCAR/MMM-physics CCPP kernel `bl_gwdo_run`).\n\nThe stress is constant from the surface up to the launch level kref. Above it the sweep asks, level by level, whether the wave is still stable. It reconstructs the wave's displacement amplitude from the stress it carries, hd = sqrt(tau / tem1), forms the wave Froude number fro = N hd / U, and evaluates Shutts's (1985) MINIMUM Richardson number of the perturbed flow, rim = Ri (1 - fro) / (1 + sqrt(Ri) fro)^2. If rim is still above the critical value the wave is stable and passes the stress on unchanged; if it has fallen to or below it the wave breaks, and the SATURATION HYPOTHESIS resets the amplitude to the largest one the flow can support, hd = U (2 sqrt(temc) - temc) / N with temc = 2 + 1/sqrt(Ri), whose stress tem1 hd^2 is what continues upward. A critical level -- Ri below critical, or a reversal of the projected wind -- terminates the wave once and for all: the flag is a running OR, so every interface above it carries zero stress.\n\nA RECURRENCE IS JUSTIFIED HERE, and it is the narrow kind esm-spec 4.3.1.1 reserves for genuinely sequential sweeps. The dependence is causal and one-directional: the amplitude at an interface is reconstructed from the stress the level below passed up, so tau[k+1] is a function of tau[k] and cannot be written as an aggregate over independent terms. But only the BRANCH TEST needs it. The saturated stress itself, tem1 hd_sat^2, depends on nothing but the local flow, so it is precomputed as an ordinary column array and the recurrence body carries just the Shutts test and the choice between three outcomes. The shared test is an expression template applied in both places, so the .esm never writes it twice.\n\nFORTRAN_BUGS N85 LIVES HERE. The saturated amplitude carries the factor 2 sqrt(temc) - temc, which is exactly zero at temc = 4, that is at Ri = 0.25 -- the SAME value the branch test `rim <= ric` compares against. So the columns that take the saturation branch marginally are precisely the ones where the factor is smallest, and a perturbation of Ri is amplified into the stress by d ln(factor) / d ln(Ri) = 1/(2 sqrt(temc) (sqrt(temc) - 1) (sqrt(Ri) + ...)), which reaches 37 at the worst column of the reference set. The stress goes as the factor SQUARED, so the amplification there is 74 in tau. It is exposed as its own observed, `satfac`, and asserted level by level wherever the branch is taken, so that a test which loses digits says WHERE it lost them. Column tolerances in this file are absolute, normalised by the profile's own maximum; a per-cell relative bound is meaningless on a quantity that passes through zero by construction.\n\nTHE TOP INTERFACE IS DROPPED TO ZERO. The Fortran's sweep runs k = 2 .. kte-1 and assigns taup(k+1), so taup(kte+1) -- the stress at the model top -- is never assigned and keeps its initialisation of zero, no matter what stress reached taup(kte) just below it. On a column whose wave survives to the top that is a step from a finite stress to nothing across one interface, and the momentum stage turns it into drag on the topmost layer. It is reproduced here, and recorded as FORTRAN_BUGS B21.",
    "license": "MIT",
    "tags": [
      "wrf",
      "gravity-wave-drag",
      "gwdo",
      "wave-saturation",
      "richardson-number",
      "recurrence",
      "column"
    ],
    "references": [
      {
        "citation": "Shutts, G. (1985). Gravity-wave drag parametrization over complex terrain: the effect of critical-level absorption in directional wind-shear. Quart. J. Roy. Meteor. Soc. 121, 1005-1021."
      },
      {
        "citation": "Kim, Y.-J., and A. Arakawa (1995). Improvement of orographic gravity wave parameterization using a mesoscale gravity wave model. J. Atmos. Sci. 52, 1875-1902.",
        "doi": "10.1175/1520-0469(1995)052<1875:IOOGWP>2.0.CO;2"
      },
      {
        "citation": "Hong, S.-Y., and co-authors (2025). Revision of the orographic gravity wave drag parameterization in the KIAPS Integrated Model. Wea. Forecasting."
      },
      {
        "citation": "NCAR/MMM-physics bl_gwdo.F90 at commit 52cfbd7 (WRF 4.8 phys/physics_mmm submodule)."
      }
    ]
  },
  "metaparameters": {
    "NLEV": {
      "type": "integer",
      "default": 50,
      "description": "Number of model layers (WRF kte); the stress profile lives on the NLEV+1 interfaces. Bound at the mount edge and passed down to the `oro` subsystem and on to the column beneath it."
    }
  },
  "models": {
    "GWDOWaveSaturation": {
      "subsystems": {
        "wrf": {
          "ref": "../../../lib/wrf_constants.esm"
        },
        "par": {
          "ref": "./gwdo_parameters.esm"
        },
        "oro": {
          "ref": "./orographic_statistics.esm",
          "bindings": {
            "NLEV": "NLEV"
          }
        }
      },
      "variables": {
        "ldrag": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "units": "1",
          "description": "The column's off switch, read from the mounted column state; 1 means no drag anywhere in this column."
        },
        "kref": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "units": "1",
          "description": "Wave-launch level index, read from the mounted column state."
        },
        "velco": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "units": "m/s",
          "shape": [
            "lev"
          ],
          "description": "Projected wind speed at interface k+1/2, read from the mounted column state. Its SIGN is what detects a critical level."
        },
        "usqj": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "units": "1",
          "shape": [
            "lev"
          ],
          "description": "Richardson-number profile as this stage sees it, read from the mounted column state: the low-level mean below kref and the raw layer value at and above it. Only the k >= kref part is read here."
        },
        "bnv2": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "units": "1/s^2",
          "shape": [
            "lev"
          ],
          "description": "Squared Brunt-Vaisala frequency profile, read from the mounted column state."
        },
        "rho": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "units": "kg/m^3",
          "shape": [
            "lev"
          ],
          "description": "Air density, read from the mounted column state."
        },
        "velco_safe": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "units": "m/s",
          "shape": [
            "lev"
          ],
          "description": "velco with its non-positive values replaced by 1 m/s. The Fortran never divides by a non-positive velco, because the critical-level flag has already terminated the wave there; the substitution is what lets the whole profile be formed as an array without a division by zero, and it can never reach an answer."
        },
        "brvf": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "units": "1/s",
          "shape": [
            "lev"
          ],
          "description": "Brunt-Vaisala frequency at interface k+1/2, sqrt(max(N^2, bnv2min))."
        },
        "tem1": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "units": "kg/(m^2*s)",
          "shape": [
            "lev"
          ],
          "description": "The coefficient that relates the wave stress to the square of its displacement amplitude, coefm/dxy (rho[k+1] + rho[k]) N U / 2. Written with the Fortran's own left-to-right association, because the stress it produces is the quantity N85 amplifies and a reassociation is a different number in the last digits. Element NLEV would gather a density above the top of the column; it is never read (the sweep's last self-read is at NLEV-1) and is set to one there."
        },
        "tem1_safe": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "units": "kg/(m^2*s)",
          "shape": [
            "lev"
          ],
          "description": "tem1 with its non-positive values replaced by 1, for the same reason as velco_safe: the amplitude sqrt(tau/tem1) is only ever asked for where the wave is still alive, and there tem1 is positive."
        },
        "srj": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "units": "1",
          "shape": [
            "lev"
          ],
          "description": "sqrt(Ri), floored at sqrt(ric). The Fortran takes this square root unguarded, which is safe only because the critical-level flag has already fired wherever Ri < ric (and Ri can be as low as rimin = -100); the floor reproduces the reachable values exactly and keeps the unreachable ones real."
        },
        "temc": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "units": "1",
          "shape": [
            "lev"
          ],
          "description": "2 + 1/sqrt(Ri), the combination whose square root the saturated amplitude is built from. It equals 4 exactly at the critical Richardson number."
        },
        "satfac": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "units": "1",
          "shape": [
            "lev"
          ],
          "description": "THE N85 QUANTITY: 2 sqrt(temc) - temc, the factor that sets the saturated wave amplitude. It vanishes at temc = 4, i.e. at Ri = ric = 0.25, which is the same value the branch test compares against -- so the levels that take the saturation branch marginally are exactly the levels where this is smallest, and the stress, which goes as its square, is correspondingly ill-conditioned there. On the worst column of the reference set it is 1.301e-2 at Ri = 0.25664, and a relative perturbation of Ri is amplified 37-fold into it."
        },
        "hd_sat": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "units": "m",
          "description": "Saturated wave displacement amplitude U (2 sqrt(temc) - temc) / N: the largest amplitude the local flow can support without the wave overturning. It is what the saturation hypothesis resets the wave to.",
          "shape": [
            "lev"
          ]
        },
        "taup_sat": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "units": "Pa",
          "shape": [
            "lev"
          ],
          "description": "The stress the wave would carry above interface k+1/2 IF it breaks there: tem1 hd_sat^2. It depends only on the local flow, not on the stress arriving from below, so it is an ordinary column array and the recurrence does not have to carry it."
        },
        "crit": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "units": "1",
          "shape": [
            "lev"
          ],
          "description": "1 at a level that terminates the wave -- Richardson number below critical, or a non-positive projected wind -- and 0 otherwise."
        },
        "ncrit": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "units": "1",
          "shape": [
            "lev"
          ],
          "description": "Number of terminating levels at or below k and at or above the launch level: a forward prefix sum of `crit` over the window [kref, k], which is empty and therefore zero below kref."
        },
        "icrilv": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "units": "1",
          "shape": [
            "lev"
          ],
          "description": "The Fortran's running critical-level flag, 1 once any level from kref up to and including k has terminated the wave. It is a prefix reduction, not a recurrence: its terms do not depend on its own result. It is defined as zero at the TOP layer because the Fortran's sweep runs only to kte-1 and never evaluates the flag there; nothing reads it at that level either, since the sweep's last self-read is one layer below."
        },
        "dead": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "units": "1",
          "shape": [
            "lev"
          ],
          "description": "1 where no stress can pass upward from level k: the column is switched off, or the wave has already met a critical level."
        },
        "taup_wave": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "units": "Pa",
          "shape": [
            "lev_nodes"
          ],
          "description": "Wave stress at the NLEV+1 interfaces, before the flow-blocking stress is added to it: taub from the surface to kref, then the saturation sweep, then zero above a critical level. The TOP interface is zero unconditionally, because the Fortran's sweep assigns taup(k+1) for k up to kte-1 only and never reaches taup(kte+1) (FORTRAN_BUGS B21)."
        },
        "fro": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "units": "1",
          "shape": [
            "lev"
          ],
          "description": "Wave Froude number N hd / U at interface k+1/2, reconstructed from the stress the profile actually carries there. Defined here for diagnosis; the sweep computes the same quantity from its own partial result through the shared `shutts_min_richardson` template."
        },
        "rim": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "units": "1",
          "shape": [
            "lev"
          ],
          "description": "Shutts's (1985) minimum Richardson number of the flow perturbed by the wave, Ri (1 - fro) / (1 + sqrt(Ri) fro)^2. The branch test of the sweep."
        },
        "tested": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "units": "1",
          "shape": [
            "lev"
          ],
          "description": "1 at each level where the Fortran actually evaluates the Shutts test: at or above the launch level, in a column with drag, with no critical level yet and a positive stress arriving from below."
        },
        "sat_code": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "units": "1",
          "shape": [
            "lev"
          ],
          "description": "The branch record of the sweep, in the diagnostic build's encoding: 0 where the test was not evaluated, 1 where the saturation hypothesis was applied, 2 where the wave passed unbroken. Asserting it pins every branch decision of the sweep at every level."
        },
        "satfac_taken": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "units": "1",
          "shape": [
            "lev"
          ],
          "description": "`satfac` at the levels where the saturation branch was actually taken, and zero elsewhere -- the form the diagnostic build publishes, so that the N85 factor can be pinned exactly where it governs."
        },
        "rim_tested": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "units": "1",
          "shape": [
            "lev"
          ],
          "description": "`rim` at the levels where it was actually evaluated, and zero elsewhere."
        }
      },
